WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
We don't speak to everyone in our lives in
the exact same way.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.660
We speak to our bosses, to customers,
and to strangers quite formally,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.019
and we speak to our friends
and family more casually.

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:13.519
We convey different aspects of ourselves
through our speech,

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:19.020
and so we change our way of speaking depending
on the identity that we’re trying to project.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.430
We do it, for the most part, unconsciously,

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.630
and through history getting it wrong could
result in anything from slight awkwardness

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:26.850
to death,

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:32.680
depending on how badly you misjudged your speech
and who exactly you were talking to at the time.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.280
What we say and how we say it matters.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.160
But registers aren’t just for spoken language:

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.800
they apply to the written word too.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.290
Now, there are people who believe all writing
on the internet should follow the same standards

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.000
as a business email.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.070
But chatting to a friend is very different
to emailing your boss,

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.510
and the way we write reflects that.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.160
Our online voice can have a wide variety of
"tones”,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.590
and especially for folks who grew up with
the internet,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:03.510
the need to convey tone means that there are
fairly well-agreed-upon conventions.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.100
In standard written English, capital letters
don’t convey much actual information.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.170
They just show proper nouns and
the beginning of sentences.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.869
And while that might make a paragraph
easier to read,

00:01:15.869 --> 00:01:19.460
we don’t flag those up at all
when we speak, it’s not necessary.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.680
So in informal conversations,
those that feel like speaking,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.590
we don’t need capital letters.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.520
But that means online communities could 
use capitals to convey

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.869
something else that does
appear in conversation: tone.

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:36.500
So all-caps became shouting.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.640
Side note: put that text in a speech bubble
and a hand-lettered font

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.620
and suddenly it’s not shouting,

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.800
it’s just how comic books look.
Context matters.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.479
Anyway: if all caps are shouting,

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:49.380
lowercase is calm, normal conversation.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.869
Remove the punctuation and you get this sort
of aloof,

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:56.500
don’t-really-care tone that works well for
deadpan humor and irony.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.450
Some people think all lowercase is "lazy,"

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.580
but it often takes work
on the part of those typing.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.100
Most smartphones autocorrect it,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.790
they capitalize proper nouns and the beginning
of sentences for you.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:11.440
Turning that off or, more likely, going back
and undoing that capitalization takes effort,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.320
and that shows that it's actually meaningful
and not just "lazy." In fact,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.650
the rise of deliberate lowercasing
for stylistic effect

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:22.130
coincides with the rise of autocapitalization
on smartphones.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:23.130
And on the other hand,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:27.480
people will sometimes capitalize the first
letter of words For Effect.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.300
That happened way before the internet:
Winnie-the-Pooh's author, A. A. Milne,

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.000
used that a long, long time ago and fairly often

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.300
in a way that seems
quite relatable today.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.650
We can repeat characters to convey prosody,
rhythm and sound, through text.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.230
We can repeat exclamation marks for more emphasis.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.190
We can use question marks, not for questions?

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:50.319
but for a rising tone? That means we might
be unsure about something?

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:55.740
Or that we want to hedge our bets about what
we’re suggesting, maybe? And reversing that,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.129
rhetorical questions can be marked by phrasing
them as a question,

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:01.060
but not having a question mark,

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.000
to show that there is no rising tone.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.600
And then there’s the period, or the full stop
in British English. Periods show falling tone.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.440
Which is pronounced at the end
of serious sentences.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:18.100
Which is why so many people perceive the period
as a marker of being very serious, or angry.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.780
There’s a difference between "ok"
and (breathes) "ok."

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.280
Which is fine, when everyone
understands that tone.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.780
One of the most extreme examples is on Tumblr,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.171
where commas instead of periods and deliberate
misspellings end up with "crytyping," which,

00:03:33.171 --> 00:03:34.790
depending on who you talk to,

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.739
is either a way to show emotional distress,

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:41.840
or a way to sarcastically show emotional distress.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.810
The conventions change fairly quickly:

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:47.250
now we don’t have to laboriously type out
text messages on old keypads,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:50.680
“cu l8r” doesn’t appear any more.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.500
There is no single “correct” way to write.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.810
There are standards and conventions and registers
for different use,

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.750
and all the varieties of internet-speak
are just other registers.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.250
They’re different, sure, but they are consistent,

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:07.360
and they’re understood by
those who use it.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.680
Just maybe don’t crytype to your boss.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.319
If you’ve ever wondered why you get ominous,
passive-aggressive,

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:17.910
ellipses at the end of text messages from
usually-older people,

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.849
that and a lot of other tales of internet
typography are in my co-author’s new book.

00:04:21.849 --> 00:04:25.690
Gretchen McCulloch's Because Internet
is on sale July 23, 2019.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.420
Links are in the description.

